## Absence, explicit negation and recursion

### What you will learn

Distinguish unsupported atoms, default negation and explicit negative atoms.

### Use absence as a condition

The screening rule checks whether evidence is absent from a candidate model. Uma has evidence and Vic does not, so only Vic passes. This use of not does not construct an explicit negative evidence atom.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    person(uma;vic).
    evidence(uma).
    passes(Person) :- person(Person), not evidence(Person).
    #show passes/1.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

[%{shown: ["passes(vic)"]}] = result.models
Course.display(result)
```

### Compare explicit alternatives

The two default-negated rules support two alternative models. Replacing a predicate with its explicit negative form changes the vocabulary: -ready is an atom, while not ready tests absence. The same stable-model choice appears in both examples.

```elixir
alternatives = Clingo.solve!("tea :- not cocoa. cocoa :- not tea.", models: :all)
[["cocoa"], ["tea"]] = Enum.sort(Enum.map(alternatives.models, & &1.shown))
explicit = Clingo.solve!("ready :- not -ready. -ready :- not ready.", models: :all)
[["-ready"], ["ready"]] = Enum.sort(Enum.map(explicit.models, & &1.shown))
Course.display(explicit)
```

### Give positive recursion a starting point

Positive recursion extends existing support. The two edges form a cycle, but reachability begins at the explicit seed. Removing that seed removes all reachable atoms, even though the edges remain.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    edge(dock,park). edge(park,dock).
    reached(dock).
    reached(To) :- reached(From), edge(From,To).
    #show reached/1.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

[%{shown: ["reached(dock)", "reached(park)"]}] = result.models
Course.display(result)
```

### Try it yourself

Remove reached(dock). from the recursive example. Predict whether the cycle can justify itself.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

Keep both edge facts. The solve remains satisfiable, but the only shown set is empty: positive recursion needs external support.

</details>

## Reason with missing evidence

Run top to bottom. Edit the ASP inside the string, then reevaluate the cell and any dependent cells. Solves close their controls automatically. [Wrapper guide](../elixir/01-getting-started.livemd#getting-started-with-clingo_elixir) · [Lesson contents](../README.md).

This example reasons about evidence. Alex and Blair each have a reason to act; only Alex has a trace linking them to the event. `implicated(Person)` requires both a reason and a trace. `cleared(Person)` requires a reason and the absence of a trace in the answer set.

The first cell shows only implicated people. Its one shown atom is `implicated(alex)`. These predicates describe the rules of our toy model, not a claim about how real evidence establishes guilt.

```elixir
~S"""
reason(alex).
reason(blair).
trace(alex).
cleared(Person) :- reason(Person), not trace(Person).
implicated(Person) :- reason(Person), trace(Person).
#show implicated/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Inspect the complete model

`not trace(Person)` is default negation: the condition holds when that trace atom is absent from the candidate answer set. It is different from an explicitly negated atom such as `-trace(Person)`.

The next program omits the output filter. Expect the two reason facts, `trace(alex)`, `implicated(alex)` and `cleared(blair)` in its only model. Facts are rules with empty bodies; the other rules add consequences of those facts.

```elixir
~S"""
reason(alex).
reason(blair).
trace(alex).
cleared(Person) :- reason(Person), not trace(Person).
implicated(Person) :- reason(Person), trace(Person).
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

`#show implicated/1.` can restore the narrower display. In application code, keep the `%Clingo.Result{}` returned by `Clingo.solve!` when you need to examine shown atoms or search status. A display filter changes presentation, not the underlying logic.

## Distinguish explicit and default negation

Run top to bottom. Edit the ASP inside the string, then reevaluate the cell and any dependent cells. Solves close their controls automatically. [Wrapper guide](../elixir/01-getting-started.livemd#getting-started-with-clingo_elixir) · [Lesson contents](../README.md).

Explicit negation writes the opposite of an atom as `-airborne(...)`. Default negation writes an absence test as `not airborne(...)`. They play different roles: a consistent answer set cannot contain both an atom and its explicit opposite.

The program describes Puck and Pip. Puck’s penguin rule supports an explicit negative conclusion. Pip’s alternative conclusions lead to two models; the display filter exposes only positive `airborne/1` atoms.

```elixir
~S"""
avian(puck).
penguin(puck).
avian(pip).
canary(pip).
airborne(Item) :- avian(Item), not -airborne(Item).
-airborne(Item) :- avian(Item), not airborne(Item).
-airborne(Item) :- penguin(Item).
#show airborne/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

One model shows `airborne(pip)` and the other has an empty shown set. The empty row is still a valid model: hidden facts and negative conclusions can exist even when the filter shows nothing. There is no model containing both positive and explicitly negative airborne atoms for the same bird.

Try removing the show directive to inspect the distinction directly.

## Follow positive cycles

Trace dependencies around each positive cycle. A cycle with no supporting fact cannot justify its own atoms. Compare that case with a cycle reached from an actual fact.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  a :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a:- a, b.
  b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b, c.
  b :- a, c.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  b :- c.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  b.
  c :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  b.
  c :- a, b.
  d :- a, b, c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- c.
  c :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b, d.
  b :- c, e.
  c :- a, e.
  d.
  e :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- c.
  c :- a.
  d.
  c :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b, d.
  b :- c, d.
  c :- a, d.
  d.
  c :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  c :- d.
  d :- c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b, c.
  b :- a, d.
  c :- d.
  d :- c.
  d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b.
  b :- a.
  c :- d.
  d :- c.
  c.
  b :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- b, c, d.
  b :- a, c, d.
  c :- d.
  d :- c.
  c.
  b :- d.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Test default negation

For each candidate answer set, interpret `not atom` as the absence of that atom. A successful candidate must also be justified by the resulting rules; absence tests alone do not supply positive support.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b, c.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- c.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b, d.
  b :- not c, d.
  d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  c :- d.
  d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b, d.
  b :- not c, d.
  c :- d.
  d :- not e.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  c :- d.
  d :- c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  c :- d.
  d :- c.
  d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  d :- b.
  b :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  d :- b.
  b :- d.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  a :- d.
  d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  a :- d.
  d.
  b :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- c, d.
  c :- b.
  d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- c, d.
  c :- b.
  d.
  c :- d.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Study mutual absence tests

Run the programs with cyclic default negation. Check the actual model sets instead of applying ordinary implication intuition. Depending on the rules, there may be alternatives or no stable model.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a, c.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- a.
  d :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- a, not b.
  c :- b, not a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  b :- not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  b :- c.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  b :- not c.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a, c.
  b :- not c.
  c :- not d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- not d.
  d :- not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a, e.
  c :- not d.
  d :- not c, f.
  e.
  f :- e.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- not d.
  d :- not c.
  e :- a, c.
  f :- a, d.
  g :- b, c.
  h :- b, d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not c.
  c :- not d.
  d :- not c.
  e :- a, c.
  e :- a, d.
  f :- not a, c.
  f :- b, not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- a.
  d :- c.
  c :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- a.
  d :- c.
  c :- d.
  d :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- d.
  d :- c.
  a :- c, d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- d.
  d :- c.
  a :- c, d.
  c.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Vary a negative cycle

Change the support or constraints around the negative cycle and observe the effect on stable models. An unsatisfiable result is a normal outcome; it is different from a parser error.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  a :- not a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not a, b.
  b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not a, b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not a, b.
  b :- c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not a, b.
  b :- not c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not a, b.
  b :- not c.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not a, b.
  a :- not a, c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not a, b.
  a :- not a, c.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- not c, b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- not c, a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- not c, a.
  c :- not c, b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- not c, a.
  d :- not d, b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- a.
  d :- not d, c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- a.
  d :- not d, c.
  c :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- not d.
  d :- not c.
  e :- not e, a, c.
  f :- not f, b, d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a :- not b.
  b :- not a.
  c :- not d.
  d :- not c.
  e :- not e, a, d.
  f :- not f, b, c.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```
